module.exports = {
  title: 'Rendering & Built-in Components',
  slug: 'rendering',
  order: 5,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'The Virtual DOM and the Render Pipeline',
      slug: 'virtual-dom-rendering',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>"What is the virtual DOM and why does Vue use it?" is asked constantly, and the answer most candidates give — "it's faster than the real DOM" — is wrong in a way that is easy to expose. A virtual DOM is a <em>cost</em>. You pay it to buy a declarative programming model. Understanding that inversion, and then explaining how Vue 3's compiler reduces the cost until it is nearly free, is one of the strongest things you can say in a Vue interview.</p>`,
        `<p>It also has practical value: the diffing algorithm explains why <code>:key</code> matters, why component-level splitting affects performance, and why "Vue is fine-grained" is a real claim rather than marketing.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A <strong>virtual DOM</strong> is a tree of plain JavaScript objects describing what the UI should look like. Each object — a <strong>vnode</strong> — records a tag, props and children.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// &lt;div class="card"&gt;&lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/div&gt; as a vnode tree\n{\n  type: 'div',\n  props: { class: 'card' },\n  children: [\n    { type: 'h1', props: null, children: 'Hello' },\n  ],\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>On every render Vue builds a new tree, compares it against the previous one, and applies only the differences to the real DOM. The comparison is the <strong>diff</strong>; applying the differences is the <strong>patch</strong>.</p>`,
        `<p>The reason this exists is not speed. Creating objects and walking two trees is obviously more work than calling <code>el.textContent = 'x'</code> once. The virtual DOM buys you something else: you get to write <em>declaratively</em> — describe the UI for a given state and never think about transitions between states — while the framework figures out the minimal imperative operations. It is a productivity trade that also happens to be fast enough.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch6-render-pipeline.svg|Pipeline from template through compiler to render function, producing a vnode tree that is diffed against the previous tree and patched into the real DOM, with patch flags marking dynamic bindings|Template → render function → vnode tree → diff → patch. Patch flags from the compiler tell the diff exactly what can change.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>The full pipeline has four stages:</p>`,
        `<ol>`,
        `<li><strong>Compile</strong> (build time) — the template is parsed into an AST, optimised, and generated into a render function.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Render</strong> (runtime) — the render function runs inside a reactive effect, reading state (which registers dependencies) and returning a vnode tree.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Diff</strong> — the new tree is compared with the previous one.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Patch</strong> — the minimal DOM operations are applied.</li>`,
        `</ol>`,
        `<p>Steps 2–4 re-run whenever a dependency changes, and only for the components that read the changed value.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The diffing rules.</strong> A general tree-diff is O(n³), which is unusable, so every framework uses heuristics that bring it to O(n):</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Different types → replace.</strong> If the tag or component type differs, Vue does not try to reconcile; it destroys the old subtree and creates the new one.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Same type → patch in place.</strong> Update the changed props and recurse into children.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Children are matched by key.</strong> Vue's list diff walks from both ends first (handling the common append, prepend and single-change cases cheaply), then builds a key→index map for the remaining middle section and computes a <em>longest increasing subsequence</em> to work out the smallest set of moves. That LIS step is why Vue's list reordering is efficient — and why a stable key is non-negotiable.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Where Vue 3 differs from a naive virtual DOM</strong> — this is the part worth memorising, because it is Vue's genuine architectural advantage:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Static hoisting.</strong> Nodes with no bindings are created once, outside the render function, and reused by reference on every render.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Patch flags.</strong> Each dynamic vnode carries a bitmask recording <em>which aspects</em> can change — <code>TEXT</code>, <code>CLASS</code>, <code>STYLE</code>, <code>PROPS</code>. The runtime checks only those and skips everything else, instead of comparing every prop.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Block trees.</strong> The compiler groups dynamic descendants into a flat array on the nearest "block" node. Diffing then walks that flat list rather than recursing through static structure. A component with one dynamic binding buried under ten static wrappers checks one node, not eleven.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Cached event handlers.</strong> Inline handlers are cached so they do not appear as changed props on every render.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>All of this is possible because a template is <em>statically analysable</em>. JSX cannot be optimised the same way because it is arbitrary JavaScript — a fact worth stating explicitly when comparing with React.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Update granularity.</strong> The component is Vue's update boundary: when state changes, the components that read it re-run their render functions. Because dependency tracking is per-property, a component that never reads the changed value is untouched — no <code>memo</code> required. React re-renders a component subtree on state change and asks you to prevent the waste manually.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>What the compiler actually produces — paste a template into the Vue SFC Playground's "JS" tab to see this for real:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- You write --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;div class="card"&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Static title&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;{{ message }}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;span :class="cls"&gt;fixed text&lt;/span&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Roughly what the compiler emits (names simplified)\nimport { createElementVNode as _c, toDisplayString as _s, openBlock, createElementBlock } from 'vue'\n\n// ① HOISTED — created once at module scope, reused every render\nconst _hoisted_1 = _c('h1', null, 'Static title', -1 /* HOISTED */)\n\nexport function render(_ctx) {\n  return (openBlock(), createElementBlock('div', { class: 'card' }, [\n    _hoisted_1,                                    // ← zero work per render\n    _c('p', null, _s(_ctx.message), 1 /* TEXT */), // ② patch flag: only text can change\n    _c('span', { class: _ctx.cls }, 'fixed text', 2 /* CLASS */), // only class\n  ]))\n}\n\n// The diff reads the flags: for &lt;p&gt; it compares text and nothing else;\n// for &lt;span&gt; it compares class and nothing else. It never touches &lt;h1&gt;.</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Why different types force a replace — and how to exploit it:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Different tags: the &lt;div&gt; subtree is destroyed, the &lt;section&gt; built fresh --&gt;\n&lt;div v-if="a"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;section v-else&gt;…&lt;/section&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Same tag: Vue patches in place and REUSES the input,\n     so text typed in one branch survives into the other --&gt;\n&lt;input v-if="loginMode" placeholder="Username"&gt;\n&lt;input v-else placeholder="Email"&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Distinct keys force separate identities → separate elements --&gt;\n&lt;input v-if="loginMode" key="username" placeholder="Username"&gt;\n&lt;input v-else key="email" placeholder="Email"&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Inspecting the machinery yourself:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { h } from 'vue'\n\n// h() is the function templates compile down to — you can call it directly\nconst vnode = h('div', { class: 'card' }, [\n  h('h1', 'Hello'),\n  h('p', { style: { color: 'red' } }, 'World'),\n])\n\nconsole.log(vnode.type)      // 'div'\nconsole.log(vnode.props)     // { class: 'card' }\nconsole.log(vnode.children)  // [ vnode, vnode ]\nconsole.log(vnode.patchFlag) // what the runtime will bother comparing</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Saying the virtual DOM is faster than the real DOM.</strong> It is not — it is an abstraction cost paid for declarativeness. The honest claim is that it is <em>fast enough</em>, and that Vue 3's compiler makes the cost small.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting element reuse across <code>v-if</code>/<code>v-else</code> with the same tag.</strong> Vue patches in place, so state like a typed value carries over. Add distinct keys if that is wrong for your case.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming a parent re-render forces children to re-render.</strong> A child re-renders only if its props changed or it reads changed state — Vue's fine-grained tracking already avoids most of the waste React needs <code>memo</code> for.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Thinking patch flags apply to hand-written render functions.</strong> They come from the compiler; a manual <code>h()</code> tree is diffed fully, which is one reason templates usually outperform hand-written render functions.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Blaming the virtual DOM for slow rendering.</strong> In practice the cost is almost always the number of components, expensive computeds, or a huge unvirtualised list — not the diff.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting that a changed <code>:key</code> means a remount.</strong> Different identity, so the old subtree is destroyed and state is lost — usually intentional, occasionally a surprise.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Prefer templates over hand-written render functions so you get the compiler optimisations for free.</li>`,
        `<li>Always give lists stable keys — the list diff's move detection depends on them entirely.</li>`,
        `<li>Split large components so a state change re-renders a smaller subtree, since the component is the update boundary.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep expensive derivation in computeds; the diff is rarely the bottleneck, but re-running heavy work in a render function is.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>v-once</code> for genuinely static-but-expensive subtrees, and <code>v-memo</code> for large lists whose rows rarely change.</li>`,
        `<li>Read the compiled output in the SFC Playground when you want to know what a template actually costs — it is the fastest way to build intuition.</li>`,
        `<li>Virtualise long lists rather than trying to make the diff cheaper; rendering ten thousand rows is the problem, not diffing them.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is the virtual DOM and why does Vue use it?"</strong><br>A: A tree of plain JavaScript objects describing the intended UI. On each render Vue builds a new tree, diffs it against the previous one and patches only the differences into the real DOM. It is not there because it is faster than direct DOM manipulation — it is strictly more work than one targeted mutation. It is there so you can program declaratively: describe the UI for a given state and let the framework derive the minimal set of operations, instead of hand-writing every transition between states.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How does the diff work?"</strong><br>A: Heuristics that reduce a general O(n³) tree diff to O(n). If two nodes have different types, Vue replaces the subtree rather than reconciling. If they match, it patches props and recurses. For keyed children it walks from both ends first — which covers appends, prepends and single edits cheaply — then builds a key-to-index map for the remaining middle and uses a longest-increasing-subsequence computation to find the minimum number of moves.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What makes Vue 3's virtual DOM faster than a naive one?"</strong><br>A: The compiler. Because templates are statically analysable, Vue hoists nodes with no bindings out of the render function so they are created once; attaches patch flags to dynamic nodes recording exactly which aspects can change, so the diff checks text or class alone rather than every prop; groups dynamic descendants into flat block arrays so the diff skips static structure entirely instead of recursing; and caches inline event handlers. JSX can't be optimised this way because it is arbitrary JavaScript.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Vue vs React on re-render granularity?"</strong><br>A: Vue tracks dependencies per property, so a state change re-runs only the render functions of components that actually read that property — a sibling reading other fields is untouched, with no memoisation. React re-renders the component and its subtree on any state change and gives you <code>memo</code>, <code>useMemo</code> and <code>useCallback</code> to opt out. Vue moves that bookkeeping into the framework; React leaves it to the developer, which is more flexible and more error-prone.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does my input keep its value when I toggle between two <code>v-if</code> branches?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Both branches render the same element type, so the diff patches in place and reuses the existing DOM node — the typed value lives on that node, not in your state. Give each branch a distinct <code>key</code> so they have different identities and Vue creates a fresh element instead of reusing one.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>vnode = plain object <code>{ type, props, children }</code>. Tree of them = virtual DOM.</li>`,
        `<li>Pipeline: <strong>compile → render → diff → patch</strong>. Render runs inside a reactive effect.</li>`,
        `<li>Virtual DOM is a <strong>cost paid for declarativeness</strong>, not a speed feature.</li>`,
        `<li>Diff heuristics: different type → replace · same type → patch · keyed children matched by key.</li>`,
        `<li>List diff: walk both ends → key→index map → <strong>LIS</strong> for minimum moves.</li>`,
        `<li>Vue 3 compiler: <strong>static hoisting · patch flags · block trees · cached handlers</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Only templates get those optimisations — hand-written <code>h()</code> is fully diffed.</li>`,
        `<li>Component = update boundary; fine-grained tracking means no <code>memo</code> needed.</li>`,
        `<li>Same tag across <code>v-if</code>/<code>v-else</code> ⇒ element reused ⇒ add <code>key</code> to force a fresh one.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Teleport',
      slug: 'teleport',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Every application eventually needs a modal, a dropdown, a toast or a tooltip — and every one of those hits the same wall: the element belongs <em>logically</em> to a deeply nested component but must render at the top of the DOM to escape <code>overflow: hidden</code>, <code>z-index</code> stacking contexts and <code>transform</code> on an ancestor.</p>`,
        `<p><code>&lt;Teleport&gt;</code> is Vue's built-in answer, and interviewers like it because explaining <em>why</em> it is needed requires real CSS knowledge. "A parent with <code>transform</code> creates a containing block, so <code>position: fixed</code> is no longer relative to the viewport" is a genuinely senior answer.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p><code>&lt;Teleport&gt;</code> renders its children into a <strong>different place in the DOM</strong> while keeping them in the same place in the component tree.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;Teleport to="body"&gt;\n  &lt;div class="modal"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/Teleport&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>That split is the whole idea and the reason it works so well:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>DOM position</strong> — moved to the target, so it escapes every ancestor's <code>overflow</code>, <code>z-index</code> and <code>transform</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Logical position</strong> — unchanged. Props, injections, emitted events and reactive state all work exactly as if it had not moved.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `{{fig:ch6-teleport.svg|Diagram showing a modal defined deep inside a component tree but rendered as a direct child of body, with props, inject and emit arrows still connecting it to its logical parent|Teleport moves the DOM node, not the component relationship — data and events still flow through the original tree.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>The CSS problems it solves</strong> are three, and knowing all three is what makes the answer complete:</p>`,
        `<ol>`,
        `<li><strong><code>overflow: hidden</code></strong> on an ancestor clips the element, however high its <code>z-index</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Stacking contexts.</strong> <code>z-index</code> is only comparable <em>within</em> a stacking context. If an ancestor creates one (via <code>opacity &lt; 1</code>, <code>transform</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>will-change</code>, <code>position</code> with a <code>z-index</code>…), your modal's <code>z-index: 9999</code> competes only with its siblings inside that context — it can never rise above an element in a higher context. This is the source of the "why is my modal behind the header?" bug that <code>z-index</code> escalation never fixes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Containing blocks.</strong> An ancestor with <code>transform</code>, <code>perspective</code> or <code>filter</code> becomes the containing block for <code>position: fixed</code> descendants — so <code>fixed</code> stops meaning "relative to the viewport" and starts meaning "relative to that ancestor." A full-screen overlay suddenly covers only part of the screen.</li>`,
        `</ol>`,
        `<p>Teleporting to <code>body</code> removes all three at once, because there are no problematic ancestors left.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The <code>to</code> prop</strong> takes a CSS selector or an actual element, and the target <em>must exist when the Teleport mounts</em>. Teleporting into another Vue component's DOM is fragile for that reason; <code>body</code> or a dedicated <code>&lt;div id="modals"&gt;</code> in <code>index.html</code> is the reliable choice.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>disabled</code></strong> toggles teleporting at runtime without unmounting: the content moves back inline, keeping component state. That is how you build something that is a modal on mobile and an inline panel on desktop.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Multiple teleports to the same target append</strong> rather than replace, in mount order — so stacked modals and toast lists work naturally.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>SSR.</strong> Teleport needs a DOM target, so on the server the content is rendered separately and hydrated into place on the client. Frameworks handle this, but a target that only exists client-side will produce a hydration mismatch — hence the <code>&lt;ClientOnly&gt;</code> wrapper you see around modals in Nuxt.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Modal.vue — buried five levels deep, renders at the top of body --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { watch, onUnmounted } from 'vue'\n\nconst open = defineModel('open', { type: Boolean })\ndefineProps({ title: String })\n\n// Lock body scroll while open — a detail real modals need\nwatch(open, (isOpen) =&gt; {\n  document.body.style.overflow = isOpen ? 'hidden' : ''\n})\nonUnmounted(() =&gt; { document.body.style.overflow = '' })\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;Teleport to="body"&gt;\n    &lt;Transition name="fade"&gt;\n      &lt;!-- .self so clicks inside the dialog don't close it --&gt;\n      &lt;div v-if="open" class="backdrop" @click.self="open = false"&gt;\n        &lt;div class="modal" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" :aria-label="title"&gt;\n          &lt;h2&gt;{{ title }}&lt;/h2&gt;\n          &lt;slot /&gt;   &lt;!-- slot content still comes from the LOGICAL parent --&gt;\n          &lt;button @click="open = false"&gt;Close&lt;/button&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/Transition&gt;\n  &lt;/Teleport&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- The parent uses it normally — v-model, props and events all work --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\nconst showModal = ref(false)\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;div class="deeply nested overflow-hidden transformed"&gt;\n    &lt;button @click="showModal = true"&gt;Open&lt;/button&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- Renders in body, but is still this component's child --&gt;\n    &lt;Modal v-model:open="showModal" title="Confirm"&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Delete this item?&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/Modal&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Conditional teleporting — modal on mobile, inline on desktop:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, onMounted, onUnmounted } from 'vue'\n\nconst isDesktop = ref(false)\nlet mq\n\nonMounted(() =&gt; {\n  mq = window.matchMedia('(min-width: 1024px)')\n  isDesktop.value = mq.matches\n  mq.addEventListener('change', (e) =&gt; { isDesktop.value = e.matches })\n})\nonUnmounted(() =&gt; mq?.removeEventListener('change', () =&gt; {}))\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- disabled toggles WITHOUT unmounting — component state survives --&gt;\n  &lt;Teleport to="body" :disabled="isDesktop"&gt;\n    &lt;FilterPanel /&gt;\n  &lt;/Teleport&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- A dedicated target beats \"body\" once you have several layers --&gt;\n&lt;!-- index.html --&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;div id="app"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div id="modals"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;    &lt;!-- always above #app in the DOM --&gt;\n  &lt;div id="toasts"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Multiple teleports to one target APPEND in mount order --&gt;\n&lt;Teleport to="#toasts"&gt;&lt;Toast v-for="t in toasts" :key="t.id" :toast="t" /&gt;&lt;/Teleport&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Target does not exist yet.</strong> Vue warns and renders nothing. The target must be in the DOM before the Teleport mounts — put it in <code>index.html</code>, not inside another Vue component that may mount later.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting scoped styles to follow.</strong> They do: the teleported content still carries the component's <code>data-v-</code> hash. But a parent's <em>descendant</em> selectors no longer match, because the element is no longer a descendant in the DOM.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Thinking the component tree changed.</strong> It did not — <code>provide</code>/<code>inject</code>, props and emits all behave as before. That is the entire point.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Escaping the stacking context and forgetting the rest.</strong> Teleport does not give you focus trapping, <code>Escape</code> handling, <code>aria-modal</code> or scroll locking. An accessible dialog needs all of them.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Hydration mismatches in SSR.</strong> If the target is only present on the client, wrap in <code>&lt;ClientOnly&gt;</code> or equivalent.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Teleporting a huge subtree unnecessarily.</strong> Use it for overlays, not as a general layout tool — it makes the DOM harder to reason about.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>disabled</code> exists</strong> and unmounting/remounting instead, which loses state and replays transitions.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Teleport to a dedicated container (<code>#modals</code>, <code>#toasts</code>) declared in <code>index.html</code> rather than to <code>body</code> directly once you have more than one overlay type.</li>`,
        `<li>Combine with <code>&lt;Transition&gt;</code> for enter/leave animations — they compose without extra work.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>disabled</code> for responsive behaviour instead of conditionally rendering two different trees.</li>`,
        `<li>Build accessibility in: <code>role="dialog"</code>, <code>aria-modal="true"</code>, a focus trap, <code>Escape</code> to close, and focus restored to the trigger on close.</li>`,
        `<li>Lock body scroll while a modal is open, and always release it in <code>onUnmounted</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep the modal's own state where it logically belongs — teleporting is a rendering decision, not a state one.</li>`,
        `<li>Wrap in <code>&lt;ClientOnly&gt;</code> under SSR when the target is client-only.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is <code>&lt;Teleport&gt;</code> and what problem does it solve?"</strong><br>A: It renders a component's children into a different part of the DOM while keeping them in the same position in the component tree. It exists because overlays have conflicting requirements: a modal belongs logically to the component that opens it, but must render outside every ancestor to escape <code>overflow: hidden</code>, stacking contexts and transformed containing blocks. Teleport separates the two — the DOM node moves, the component relationship doesn't, so props, injections and emits all still work.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why can't you just use a high <code>z-index</code>?"</strong><br>A: Because <code>z-index</code> only orders elements <em>within</em> the same stacking context. If any ancestor creates one — <code>opacity</code> below 1, a <code>transform</code>, a <code>filter</code>, <code>will-change</code>, or positioning with its own <code>z-index</code> — then your modal's <code>z-index: 9999</code> is compared against its siblings inside that context, not against the page. It can never rise above an element in a higher context, no matter how large the number. And <code>overflow: hidden</code> clips it regardless of <code>z-index</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "My fixed-position overlay only covers part of the screen. Why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: An ancestor has <code>transform</code>, <code>perspective</code> or <code>filter</code> applied, which makes it the containing block for <code>position: fixed</code> descendants. <code>fixed</code> then positions relative to that ancestor rather than the viewport. It's a common surprise inside animated or transformed layouts, and teleporting to <code>body</code> removes the offending ancestor from the chain entirely.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Does teleported content still receive provide/inject?"</strong><br>A: Yes. Teleport only changes where the DOM nodes are inserted; the component hierarchy is untouched, so injection resolution, prop passing, emitted events and reactive state all behave exactly as if the content had rendered in place. Scoped style attributes travel with it too — but a parent's descendant selectors stop matching, because the element is no longer a DOM descendant.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the <code>disabled</code> prop for?"</strong><br>A: Toggling teleporting at runtime without unmounting. The content moves back to its original inline position and keeps all its state, which is how you make a panel a full-screen modal on mobile and an inline sidebar on desktop with one component and one media query, rather than rendering two separate trees and losing state on every breakpoint change.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;Teleport to="body"&gt;</code> — moves the <strong>DOM position</strong>, keeps the <strong>component position</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Solves: ancestor <code>overflow: hidden</code> · stacking contexts · transformed containing blocks.</li>`,
        `<li>Stacking context creators: <code>opacity&lt;1</code>, <code>transform</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>will-change</code>, positioned + <code>z-index</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>transform</code>/<code>perspective</code>/<code>filter</code> on an ancestor breaks <code>position: fixed</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>props / emits / provide-inject / scoped-style hashes all still work.</li>`,
        `<li><code>:disabled</code> toggles teleporting <strong>without unmounting</strong> — state preserved.</li>`,
        `<li>Target must exist at mount; prefer <code>#modals</code> in <code>index.html</code> over <code>body</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Multiple teleports to one target <strong>append</strong> in mount order.</li>`,
        `<li>Composes with <code>&lt;Transition&gt;</code>. SSR needs <code>&lt;ClientOnly&gt;</code> if the target is client-only.</li>`,
        `<li>Teleport ≠ accessibility: add focus trap, <code>Escape</code>, <code>aria-modal</code>, scroll lock.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Transitions and Animations',
      slug: 'transitions',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Animation is where a competent app starts feeling finished, and Vue ships a genuinely good primitive for it. Interviewers ask about <code>&lt;Transition&gt;</code> because it requires understanding <em>when</em> Vue can hook into an element's life — you cannot fade out an element that <code>v-if</code> has already removed, so the framework has to defer the removal until the animation ends. Explaining that mechanism shows you understand the rendering lifecycle.</p>`,
        `<p>The practical value is high too: enter/leave animations, list reordering with FLIP, and route transitions are standard requirements, and hand-rolling them is fiddly and easy to get wrong.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p><code>&lt;Transition&gt;</code> wraps a <em>single</em> element or component and applies CSS classes at the right moments when it enters or leaves.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;Transition name="fade"&gt;\n  &lt;p v-if="show"&gt;Hello&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/Transition&gt;\n\n&lt;style&gt;\n.fade-enter-active, .fade-leave-active { transition: opacity 0.3s ease; }\n.fade-enter-from,   .fade-leave-to     { opacity: 0; }\n&lt;/style&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Six classes, three per direction, and the naming is completely mechanical:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Applied</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td><code>v-enter-from</code></td><td>Before insert, removed after one frame</td><td>The starting style</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>v-enter-active</code></td><td>The whole entering phase</td><td>The <code>transition</code> property itself</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>v-enter-to</code></td><td>One frame after insert, until finished</td><td>The ending style</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>v-leave-from</code></td><td>Immediately on leave</td><td>The starting style</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>v-leave-active</code></td><td>The whole leaving phase</td><td>The <code>transition</code> property</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>v-leave-to</code></td><td>One frame after leave starts</td><td>The ending style</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>The <code>v-</code> prefix becomes your <code>name</code> — <code>name="fade"</code> gives <code>fade-enter-from</code> and so on.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch6-transition-classes.svg|Timeline of the six transition classes across the enter and leave phases, showing enter-from applied before insertion and swapped for enter-to after one frame, and the element's removal deferred until the leave transition completes|The class timeline: Vue swaps -from for -to after one frame to start the transition, and delays DOM removal until it finishes.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Entering.</strong> Vue inserts the element with <code>enter-from</code> and <code>enter-active</code> applied, forces a reflow so the browser registers that starting state, then on the next frame swaps <code>enter-from</code> for <code>enter-to</code>. The change of properties with a <code>transition</code> in force is what animates. When the <code>transitionend</code> event fires, the remaining classes are removed.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Leaving</strong> is the interesting half, because it is what you cannot do yourself easily: Vue applies <code>leave-from</code> and <code>leave-active</code>, swaps in <code>leave-to</code> after a frame, and <strong>delays the actual DOM removal</strong> until the transition completes. This is why <code>&lt;Transition&gt;</code> exists — with a bare <code>v-if</code> the element vanishes instantly and there is nothing left to animate.</p>`,
        `<p>Vue auto-detects the duration from the CSS, so you rarely specify it. Explicit <code>:duration</code> is the escape hatch when nested transitions confuse the detection.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Modes</strong> control overlap when one element replaces another. By default both animate simultaneously, which usually looks wrong. <code>mode="out-in"</code> — the outgoing element leaves, <em>then</em> the incoming one enters — is what you want almost every time, and is the standard choice for route transitions.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>&lt;TransitionGroup&gt;</code></strong> is the list version, and it does something <code>&lt;Transition&gt;</code> does not: it animates <em>reordering</em> using the FLIP technique. Vue records each element's position before the update (First), reads the new position after (Last), computes the delta and applies a <code>transform</code> to put it visually back where it started (Invert), then transitions the transform away (Play). Because it animates <code>transform</code> rather than layout properties, it is GPU-accelerated and does not trigger reflow. Applying a <code>v-move</code> class is all you have to do.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>JavaScript hooks</strong> — <code>@enter</code>, <code>@leave</code> and friends — give you the element and a <code>done</code> callback for library-driven animation (GSAP, anime.js). Setting <code>:css="false"</code> tells Vue to skip class handling entirely and rely on your <code>done()</code> call.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- Basic fade --&gt;\n  &lt;Transition name="fade"&gt;\n    &lt;p v-if="show"&gt;Hello&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/Transition&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Different enter and leave, plus appear on initial render --&gt;\n  &lt;Transition name="slide" appear&gt;\n    &lt;aside v-if="open" class="drawer"&gt;…&lt;/aside&gt;\n  &lt;/Transition&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- out-in: the outgoing element finishes before the incoming starts --&gt;\n  &lt;Transition name="fade" mode="out-in"&gt;\n    &lt;component :is="currentTab" :key="currentTab" /&gt;\n  &lt;/Transition&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Reuse an animation library's classes instead of writing your own --&gt;\n  &lt;Transition\n    enter-active-class="animate__animated animate__fadeInDown"\n    leave-active-class="animate__animated animate__fadeOutUp"\n  &gt;\n    &lt;div v-if="show"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/Transition&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;style&gt;\n.fade-enter-active, .fade-leave-active { transition: opacity 0.3s ease; }\n.fade-enter-from,   .fade-leave-to     { opacity: 0; }\n\n/* Asymmetric: slide in from the left, fade out */\n.slide-enter-active { transition: transform 0.3s cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1); }\n.slide-leave-active { transition: opacity 0.2s ease; }\n.slide-enter-from   { transform: translateX(-100%); }\n.slide-leave-to     { opacity: 0; }\n&lt;/style&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p><code>&lt;TransitionGroup&gt;</code> — including the move animation that is its real selling point:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- tag= renders a real wrapper element; without it there is none --&gt;\n  &lt;TransitionGroup name="list" tag="ul" class="todo-list"&gt;\n    &lt;!-- :key is REQUIRED — this is how FLIP tracks each element --&gt;\n    &lt;li v-for="todo in sortedTodos" :key="todo.id"&gt;{{ todo.text }}&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/TransitionGroup&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;style&gt;\n.list-enter-active, .list-leave-active { transition: all 0.4s ease; }\n.list-enter-from, .list-leave-to { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(30px); }\n\n/* ① Animates REORDERING via FLIP — transform only, so it's GPU-friendly */\n.list-move { transition: transform 0.4s ease; }\n\n/* ② Take the leaving element out of layout flow, or the surviving items\n      jump instantly instead of sliding into place. */\n.list-leave-active { position: absolute; }\n&lt;/style&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>JavaScript hooks, for library-driven animation:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport gsap from 'gsap'\n\nfunction onEnter(el, done) {\n  gsap.fromTo(el,\n    { opacity: 0, y: 30 },\n    { opacity: 1, y: 0, duration: 0.4, onComplete: done },  // ← must call done()\n  )\n}\nfunction onLeave(el, done) {\n  gsap.to(el, { opacity: 0, y: -30, duration: 0.3, onComplete: done })\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- :css=\"false\" skips class handling entirely; Vue waits for done() --&gt;\n  &lt;Transition :css="false" @enter="onEnter" @leave="onLeave"&gt;\n    &lt;div v-if="show"&gt;Animated by GSAP&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/Transition&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Route transitions — the pattern you will reach for most:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;RouterView v-slot="{ Component, route }"&gt;\n    &lt;Transition :name="route.meta.transition || 'fade'" mode="out-in"&gt;\n      &lt;component :is="Component" :key="route.path" /&gt;\n    &lt;/Transition&gt;\n  &lt;/RouterView&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Multiple root elements inside <code>&lt;Transition&gt;</code>.</strong> It handles exactly one at a time; use <code>&lt;TransitionGroup&gt;</code> for lists.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No <code>:key</code> in <code>&lt;TransitionGroup&gt;</code>.</strong> FLIP has nothing to track, so nothing animates.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>position: absolute</code> on <code>-leave-active</code> in a group.</strong> The leaving element still occupies layout space, so the others snap instead of sliding.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting <code>v-show</code>-only transitions to work identically.</strong> They do work, but with <code>v-show</code> the element stays in the DOM, so leave animations run on a still-present node — occasionally a difference that matters for layout.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Missing <code>mode="out-in"</code>.</strong> Both elements animate at once and overlap, which almost never looks intentional.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>done()</code> in a JS hook.</strong> Vue waits forever and the element never finishes leaving.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Animating layout properties.</strong> <code>height</code>, <code>width</code>, <code>top</code> and <code>margin</code> trigger reflow on every frame. Prefer <code>transform</code> and <code>opacity</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No <code>appear</code> when you wanted an entrance on first render.</strong> By default nothing animates on initial mount.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Ignoring <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.</strong> Vestibular disorders are real; wrap your transitions in the media query.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Animate <code>transform</code> and <code>opacity</code> only — they are composited and do not trigger layout or paint.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>mode="out-in"</code> whenever one element replaces another.</li>`,
        `<li>Always key <code>&lt;TransitionGroup&gt;</code> children and add a <code>-move</code> class to get reordering for free.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep durations short — 150–300ms for UI feedback, up to 400ms for larger movements. Longer feels sluggish.</li>`,
        `<li>Respect <code>@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)</code> and disable or shorten transitions there.</li>`,
        `<li>Name transitions semantically (<code>page</code>, <code>modal</code>, <code>list</code>) and define them once in a shared stylesheet.</li>`,
        `<li>Use JavaScript hooks with <code>:css="false"</code> only when you genuinely need a library; CSS handles most cases with less code.</li>`,
        `<li>Animate to communicate — entering, leaving, reordering, state change — not for decoration.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How does <code>&lt;Transition&gt;</code> work?"</strong><br>A: It applies six CSS classes at the right moments. Entering: the element is inserted with <code>-enter-from</code> and <code>-enter-active</code>, Vue forces a reflow so the browser registers the starting state, then swaps <code>-enter-from</code> for <code>-enter-to</code> on the next frame, which starts the transition. Leaving is the important half — Vue applies <code>-leave-from</code>/<code>-leave-active</code>, swaps in <code>-leave-to</code>, and <em>delays removing the element from the DOM</em> until the transition finishes. That deferral is the whole reason the component exists, because a plain <code>v-if</code> removes the node instantly and leaves nothing to animate.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What does <code>&lt;TransitionGroup&gt;</code> add?"</strong><br>A: Two things: it handles multiple elements, and it animates <em>reordering</em> with FLIP. Vue records each keyed element's position before the update, reads the new position after, applies a transform that visually returns it to the old position, then transitions that transform to zero. Because it animates <code>transform</code> rather than layout, it's GPU-accelerated. You just add a <code>-move</code> class. Keys are mandatory — they're how each element is tracked across the update — and leaving elements usually need <code>position: absolute</code> so the survivors slide rather than snap.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is <code>mode="out-in"</code> for?"</strong><br>A: Sequencing. By default the outgoing and incoming elements transition simultaneously and overlap, which for a page or tab change looks like a glitch. <code>out-in</code> lets the old element finish leaving before the new one starts entering; <code>in-out</code> does the reverse and is rarely what you want. For route transitions, <code>out-in</code> is effectively the default choice.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why do you animate <code>transform</code> instead of <code>top</code> or <code>height</code>?"</strong><br>A: Because <code>transform</code> and <code>opacity</code> can be handled by the compositor — they don't trigger layout or paint, so they run on the GPU and can hit 60fps even under load. Animating <code>height</code>, <code>width</code>, <code>top</code> or <code>margin</code> forces a reflow on every frame, which is the usual cause of janky animations. It's also why FLIP is built on transforms.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "My list items snap into place instead of sliding when one is removed. Why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: The leaving element is still in the layout flow while it animates out, so the remaining items reflow instantly to fill the gap and there's nothing left to animate. Add <code>position: absolute</code> to the <code>-leave-active</code> class to take it out of flow — then the surviving items' FLIP transitions can actually play.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Six classes: <code>-enter-from</code> / <code>-enter-active</code> / <code>-enter-to</code>, same for <code>leave</code>. Prefix = <code>name</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Vue <strong>delays DOM removal</strong> until the leave transition ends — that's the point.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;Transition&gt;</code> = one element. <code>&lt;TransitionGroup&gt;</code> = many + <strong>FLIP</strong> move animation.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;TransitionGroup&gt;</code>: keys required · <code>tag="ul"</code> for a wrapper · <code>-move</code> class · <code>position:absolute</code> on leave.</li>`,
        `<li><code>mode="out-in"</code> for replacements; <code>appear</code> for the initial render.</li>`,
        `<li>Animate <strong><code>transform</code> + <code>opacity</code></strong> only. Never <code>height</code>/<code>top</code>/<code>margin</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>JS hooks <code>@enter</code>/<code>@leave</code> + <code>:css="false"</code> ⇒ you must call <code>done()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Duration auto-detected; <code>:duration</code> to override.</li>`,
        `<li>Route transitions: <code>&lt;RouterView v-slot&gt;</code> + <code>&lt;Transition mode="out-in"&gt;</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Honour <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Async Components, Suspense and KeepAlive',
      slug: 'async-suspense-keepalive',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>These three built-ins solve the problems that appear once an app is real: the bundle is too big, loading states are scattered across every component, and switching tabs throws away work the user did. Interviewers ask about them because they connect Vue knowledge to performance and UX — <code>defineAsyncComponent</code> is a bundle-size question, <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> is a loading-state-architecture question, and <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> is a lifecycle question with a memory-leak trap in it.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p><strong><code>defineAsyncComponent</code></strong> defers loading a component's code until it is first rendered. The bundler splits it into a separate chunk, which leaves the initial bundle.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const HeavyChart = defineAsyncComponent(() =&gt; import('./HeavyChart.vue'))</code></pre>`,
        `<p><strong><code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code></strong> is a boundary that shows a fallback while any async dependency <em>inside it</em> resolves — async components, or components using top-level <code>await</code> in <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>. It centralises the loading state instead of each component owning one.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code></strong> caches component instances instead of destroying them, so state survives being switched away from and back.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch6-async-suspense-keepalive.svg|Three panels: defineAsyncComponent splitting a chunk loaded on demand, Suspense showing a fallback until nested async dependencies resolve, and KeepAlive caching instances with activated and deactivated hooks|Three separate jobs: split the bundle, centralise the loading state, and preserve instances instead of destroying them.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Async components.</strong> The loader function returns a promise; Vue renders nothing (or a loading component) until it resolves, then renders the real component. The object form adds real ergonomics: <code>loadingComponent</code> with a <code>delay</code> — so a fast load does not flash a spinner — plus <code>errorComponent</code>, a <code>timeout</code>, and <code>onError</code> for retry logic. Because <code>import()</code> is a dynamic import, the bundler creates a separate chunk automatically; no configuration required.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code></strong> has two slots, <code>#default</code> and <code>#fallback</code>. It walks its default subtree looking for async dependencies and shows the fallback until <em>all</em> of them resolve — so several async children produce one coordinated loading state rather than a cascade of spinners. It also emits <code>pending</code>, <code>resolve</code> and <code>fallback</code> events. Errors from inside are caught with <code>onErrorCaptured</code> on an ancestor, which is how you pair it with an error boundary. Note that it remains marked <strong>experimental</strong> in Vue 3 — its API could still change, which is worth saying out loud in an interview.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code></strong> intercepts unmounting: instead of destroying the component it moves the instance to a cache. The lifecycle changes accordingly — <code>onMounted</code> and <code>onUnmounted</code> fire only once, and <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code> fire on every show and hide. This is the single most important thing to know about it, because a subscription or interval cleaned up in <code>onUnmounted</code> under a <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> is never cleaned up at all.</p>`,
        `<p><code>include</code>, <code>exclude</code> (matching on component <code>name</code>) and <code>:max</code> (an LRU cache) bound what is retained. Without <code>:max</code>, a wizard the user browses through can retain every step forever — a leak with good intentions.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { defineAsyncComponent } from 'vue'\nimport LoadingSpinner from './LoadingSpinner.vue'\nimport ErrorPanel from './ErrorPanel.vue'\n\n// Simple: one chunk, loaded on first render\nconst HeavyChart = defineAsyncComponent(() =&gt; import('./HeavyChart.vue'))\n\n// Full control\nconst RichEditor = defineAsyncComponent({\n  loader: () =&gt; import('./RichEditor.vue'),\n  loadingComponent: LoadingSpinner,\n  delay: 200,        // don't flash the spinner if it loads in under 200ms\n  errorComponent: ErrorPanel,\n  timeout: 10000,    // fail after 10s\n  onError(error, retry, fail, attempts) {\n    if (attempts &lt;= 3 &amp;&amp; error.message.match(/fetch/)) retry()   // network blip\n    else fail()\n  },\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Suspense: ONE loading state for a whole subtree --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;Suspense&gt;\n    &lt;template #default&gt;\n      &lt;!-- All three may be async; the fallback shows until ALL resolve --&gt;\n      &lt;div class="dashboard"&gt;\n        &lt;UserProfile /&gt;\n        &lt;RevenueChart /&gt;\n        &lt;ActivityFeed /&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/template&gt;\n\n    &lt;template #fallback&gt;\n      &lt;DashboardSkeleton /&gt;\n    &lt;/template&gt;\n  &lt;/Suspense&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- UserProfile.vue — top-level await makes this an async component --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { onMounted, inject } from 'vue'\n\n// ⚠️ Register hooks and injections BEFORE the await —\n//    the instance context is lost across it.\nconst api = inject('api')\nonMounted(() =&gt; track('profile-view'))\n\nconst user = await api.getUser()   // suspends until resolved\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- no v-if=\"loading\" needed — Suspense owns that --&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;{{ user.name }}&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Suspense + error boundary + transition, the production combination --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, onErrorCaptured } from 'vue'\nconst error = ref(null)\nonErrorCaptured((e) =&gt; { error.value = e; return false })\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;ErrorPanel v-if="error" :error="error" @retry="error = null" /&gt;\n  &lt;Suspense v-else @pending="loading = true" @resolve="loading = false"&gt;\n    &lt;template #default&gt;&lt;AsyncDashboard /&gt;&lt;/template&gt;\n    &lt;template #fallback&gt;&lt;Skeleton /&gt;&lt;/template&gt;\n  &lt;/Suspense&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p><code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> — and the lifecycle change that comes with it:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- Tabs whose form state survives switching --&gt;\n  &lt;KeepAlive :max="5"&gt;\n    &lt;component :is="tabs[current]" /&gt;\n  &lt;/KeepAlive&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Cache only named components --&gt;\n  &lt;KeepAlive :include="['SearchResults', 'FilterPanel']"&gt;\n    &lt;component :is="view" /&gt;\n  &lt;/KeepAlive&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Keep list scroll position while detail pages come and go --&gt;\n  &lt;RouterView v-slot="{ Component }"&gt;\n    &lt;KeepAlive :include="['ProductList']"&gt;\n      &lt;component :is="Component" /&gt;\n    &lt;/KeepAlive&gt;\n  &lt;/RouterView&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { onMounted, onUnmounted, onActivated, onDeactivated, ref } from 'vue'\n\nconst scrollY = ref(0)\n\n// Under KeepAlive these fire ONCE, ever\nonMounted(() =&gt; console.log('created — one time only'))\nonUnmounted(() =&gt; console.log('destroyed — only when evicted from the cache'))\n\n// These fire on EVERY show/hide — put per-visit work here\nonActivated(() =&gt; {\n  window.scrollTo(0, scrollY.value)   // restore\n  startPolling()\n})\nonDeactivated(() =&gt; {\n  scrollY.value = window.scrollY      // remember\n  stopPolling()                       // ← in onUnmounted this would never run\n})\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Cleaning up in <code>onUnmounted</code> under <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code>.</strong> The component is deactivated, not destroyed, so timers and subscriptions keep running forever. Use <code>onDeactivated</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Unbounded <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code>.</strong> Every visited component is retained. Always set <code>:max</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>include</code>/<code>exclude</code> silently not matching.</strong> They match the component's <code>name</code>; with <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> the name is inferred from the filename, so a rename breaks the match. Set it explicitly with <code>defineOptions({ name })</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Top-level <code>await</code> without a <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> ancestor.</strong> Vue warns and the component does not render.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Registering hooks after a top-level <code>await</code>.</strong> Silently ignored — the instance context is gone.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Async-importing a component used on the initial screen.</strong> You have added a round trip to the critical path for no benefit; only split what is conditional or below the fold.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>delay</code>.</strong> A spinner that appears for 80ms reads as a flicker; <code>delay: 200</code> avoids it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Treating <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> as stable API.</strong> It is still marked experimental; say so if asked.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>One <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> around everything.</strong> The slowest child gates the whole page. Place boundaries where partial content is genuinely useful.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Async-load anything heavy and conditional: modals, rich text editors, charts, maps, admin panels.</li>`,
        `<li>Route-level code splitting is the highest-value split — lazy-import every route component.</li>`,
        `<li>Always pair <code>loadingComponent</code> with <code>delay</code>, and <code>errorComponent</code> with a retry path.</li>`,
        `<li>Place <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> boundaries around meaningful units, not around the whole app, so fast sections render immediately.</li>`,
        `<li>Pair every <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> with an <code>onErrorCaptured</code> boundary — a rejected promise inside is otherwise an unhandled error.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> where re-entry should feel instant: tab panels, list-then-detail navigation, multi-step wizards.</li>`,
        `<li>Bound the cache with <code>:max</code> and move per-visit work to <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer skeletons over spinners in <code>#fallback</code> — they communicate layout and reduce perceived wait.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you reduce the initial bundle size in a Vue app?"</strong><br>A: Code splitting, mostly at two levels. Route level: lazy-import every route component so each page is its own chunk. Component level: wrap heavy, conditionally rendered components in <code>defineAsyncComponent(() =&gt; import('./X.vue'))</code> — editors, charts, maps, modals. The dynamic import is what tells the bundler to emit a separate chunk. Beyond that, audit dependencies with rollup-plugin-visualizer, prefer tree-shakeable libraries, and don't split things that appear on first paint, because that just adds a round trip.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code>?"</strong><br>A: A boundary that shows a fallback until every async dependency in its default slot resolves — async components and components using top-level <code>await</code> in <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>. It centralises loading state: instead of each child owning a <code>loading</code> ref and a spinner, one boundary coordinates them so you get a single skeleton rather than a cascade of spinners. It's still marked experimental in Vue 3, and errors inside need an <code>onErrorCaptured</code> ancestor to be handled.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What does <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> change about the lifecycle?"</strong><br>A: It caches instances instead of destroying them, so <code>onMounted</code> and <code>onUnmounted</code> fire once rather than on every switch, and <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code> fire on every show and hide instead. That's the trap: an interval or subscription cleaned up in <code>onUnmounted</code> is never cleaned up while the component sits in the cache, so it runs forever. Per-visit setup and teardown belong in the activated/deactivated pair.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does <code>delay</code> exist on an async component's loading state?"</strong><br>A: To avoid a spinner flash. If the chunk loads in 80ms, showing and hiding a spinner reads as a glitch and feels <em>slower</em> than showing nothing. <code>delay: 200</code> means the loading component only appears if the load is genuinely slow enough for the user to notice waiting.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "My <code>include</code> list on <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> isn't matching. Why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: It matches the component's <code>name</code> option, not the file path or the import binding. With <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> the name is inferred from the filename, so renaming the file silently breaks the match, and a build that mangles names can too. Set it explicitly with <code>defineOptions({ name: 'ProductList' })</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>defineAsyncComponent(() =&gt; import('./X.vue'))</code> → separate chunk, loaded on first render.</li>`,
        `<li>Object form: <code>loader</code> · <code>loadingComponent</code> + <code>delay</code> · <code>errorComponent</code> · <code>timeout</code> · <code>onError(err, retry, fail, attempts)</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> = <code>#default</code> + <code>#fallback</code>; waits for <strong>all</strong> async deps. Still <strong>experimental</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Top-level <code>await</code> in <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> ⇒ async component ⇒ needs Suspense; register hooks <strong>before</strong> the await.</li>`,
        `<li>Pair Suspense with <code>onErrorCaptured</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> caches instances: <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code> replace mount/unmount per switch.</li>`,
        `<li>Cleanup in <code>onDeactivated</code>, not <code>onUnmounted</code>. Always set <code>:max</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>include</code>/<code>exclude</code> match the component <strong>name</strong> — set it with <code>defineOptions</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Split routes first; never async-load what's on the first paint.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
}
